WEBVTT

00:00:00.135 --> 00:00:03.992
A useful way to read requirements is to look for different kinds of language.

00:00:03.992 --> 00:00:06.512
Nouns often suggest candidate entities.

00:00:06.512 --> 00:00:09.112
Descriptive facts often become attributes.

00:00:09.112 --> 00:00:11.412
Verbs often reveal relationships.

00:00:11.412 --> 00:00:14.902
Words such as must, may, only, and at least often reveal constraints or optionality.

00:00:14.902 --> 00:00:17.537
For example, patient and provider are nouns.

00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:18.802
Birth date is a descriptive fact about a patient.

00:00:18.802 --> 00:00:20.937
A patient books an appointment describes a relationship.

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:26.602
Every appointment must have one provider describes a required relationship.

00:00:26.602 --> 00:00:27.282
Do not start by typing CREATE TABLE.

00:00:27.282 --> 00:00:30.947
Start by writing the business meaning in plain language.

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:34.952
Once the meaning is clear, translating it into a relational structure becomes much easier.

00:00:34.952 --> 00:00:39.702
Connect this idea to a clinic system separating patients, encounters, clinicians, and billing.

00:00:39.702 --> 00:00:43.302
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:43.302 --> 00:00:49.842
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:49.842 --> 00:00:53.152
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:53.152 --> 00:00:56.800
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
